@* ColorPicker's Overview, written by hand after TextField's: presets, saving a color (previewed in
   each mode), empty values, states and its own words. Accessibility and See also come from the doc
   comment. *@

<Heading Level="2">Usage</Heading>
<Code Block Language="razor">@Usage</Code>
<TextBlock>A color picker is a field for choosing a color: a swatch that opens the browser's color panel. It suits a color the user chooses freely, such as a brand color. Its value is a hex string such as <Code>#2563eb</Code>. For a few named choices, such as a label's color, a <Anchor Href="docs/components/radio-group">RadioGroup</Anchor> or a <Anchor Href="docs/components/segmented-control">SegmentedControl</Anchor> can be clearer.</TextBlock>
<TextBlock>Underneath is a native color input, so it posts with the form and opens the browser's color panel <Anchor Href="docs/common/render-modes">with JavaScript off</Anchor> too. IzzyBlazor's script adds a hex box beside the swatch, and shows the presets as swatches under it.</TextBlock>

<Heading Level="2">Presets</Heading>
<TextBlock>Presets are the colors offered first, as swatches the user clicks. Set <Code>Presets</Code> to their hex strings. To name them, set <Code>PresetText</Code>: screen readers hear "Teal" rather than "#0f766e", and the name is the swatch's tooltip.</TextBlock>
<ExampleViewer Example="color-picker-presets" />
<TextBlock>The user can still pick or type any color. If the value must be one of the presets, check it on the server.</TextBlock>

<Heading Level="2">Saving a color</Heading>
<TextBlock>The picker binds to a string. The browser sends the color as <Code>#rrggbb</Code> in lowercase, whether it was picked, chosen from a preset or typed; <Code>#F60</Code> becomes <Code>#ff6600</Code>.</TextBlock>
<ExampleViewer Example="color-picker-label-color" />
<List>
    <ListItem>The hex box accepts three or six hex digits, with or without the <Code>#</Code>. Anything else is put back to the last valid value when the box loses focus.</ListItem>
    <ListItem>On static pages, the property gets the posted text as it is, and a crafted form post can send any text. Before you save or use it, pass it through <Code>ColorValues.NormalizeHex</Code>, which returns <Code>#rrggbb</Code> or null, as this example does.</ListItem>
</List>

<Heading Level="2">Empty values</Heading>
<TextBlock>A color input always has a value. A property that's null or not a color shows black, and the form posts <Code>#000000</Code>. Start the property with a color, and treat black as a choice like any other.</TextBlock>

<Heading Level="2">Sizes, swatch only and locked</Heading>
<TextBlock>The size lines the picker up with other fields; set <Code>Size</Code> to choose one. To show the swatch alone, set <Code>ShowHex="false"</Code>. For a value the user can see but not change, set <Code>ReadOnly</Code>; it still posts. For a value that doesn't post, set <Code>Disabled</Code>.</TextBlock>
<ExampleViewer Example="color-picker-states" />
<List>
    <ListItem>A read-only picker still shows its hex box, because that's the part that can be focused.</ListItem>
    <ListItem>A read-only color posts from a hidden field. Before trusting a posted value, see <Anchor Href="docs/common/forms#values-a-form-post-can-change">Values a form post can change</Anchor>.</ListItem>
    <ListItem>A disabled picker posts nothing. After a form post on static pages, its property keeps the value a new model starts with; if that's null, the field shows black. Before saving a disabled value from a form post, see <Anchor Href="docs/common/forms#disabled-fields-and-form-posts">Disabled fields and form posts</Anchor>.</ListItem>
</List>

<Heading Level="2">Labels and localization</Heading>
<TextBlock>The hex box and the presets group have names for screen readers. To set them for the whole app, see <Anchor Href="docs/common/text">Text and other languages</Anchor>.</TextBlock>
<Prose Class="site-table ib-scroll">
    <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Parameter</th><th>Sets</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr><td><Code>HexLabel</Code></td><td>The end of the hex box's name</td><td>"Brand color, hex"</td></tr>
            <tr><td><Code>PresetsLabel</Code></td><td>The presets group's name</td><td>"Preset colors"</td></tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>
</Prose>

<Heading Level="2">Mode support</Heading>
<ModeSupportTable Component="Component" ApiComponent="ApiComponent" />

@if (ApiComponent.Accessibility is not null)
{
    <Heading Level="2">Accessibility</Heading>
    <DocHtml Html="@ApiComponent.Accessibility" Context="@(Context with { Overview = true })" Size="ProseSize.Md" />
}

<Heading Level="2">Parameters</Heading>
<ParameterTable ApiComponent="ApiComponent" ApiHref="@Registry.ApiPageHref(Component)" Context="Context" Compact />

<OverviewSeeAlso Component="Component" ApiComponent="ApiComponent" />

@code {
    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public RegistryComponent Component { get; set; } = null!;

    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public ApiComponent ApiComponent { get; set; } = null!;

    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public ApiPageContext Context { get; set; } = null!;

    private const string Usage = """
        @* A hex string, with a few colors offered first. *@
        <ColorPicker @bind-Value="Model!.Brand" Presets="@(["#2563eb", "#0f766e", "#be123c"])" />

        @* The swatch alone, for a compact row. *@
        <ColorPicker @bind-Value="Model!.Highlight" ShowHex="false" />
        """;
}
